Below is a simple HTML website that includes JavaScript to display a random joke from a list of four jokes when a button is clicked.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Random Joke</title>
    <style>
        body {
            font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
            text-align: center;
        }
        #joke {
            margin: 20px;
            padding: 10px;
            border: 1px solid #ddd;
            border-radius: 5px;
            box-shadow: 0 0 10px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Random Joke Generator</h1>
    <button onclick="showJoke()">Click for a Joke!</button>
    <div id="joke"></div>

    <script>
        const jokes = [
            "Why don't scientists trust atoms? Because they make up everything.",
            "Why don't eggs tell jokes? They'd crack each other up.",
            "Why did the tomato turn red? Because it saw the salad dressing.",
            "What do you call a fake noodle? An impasta."
        ];

        function showJoke() {
            const jokeElement = document.getElementById('joke');
            const randomJoke = jokes[Math.floor(Math.random() * jokes.length)];
            jokeElement.innerText = randomJoke;
        }
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

This code does the following:
- Defines a list of jokes in a JavaScript array.
- Creates a button that, when clicked, calls the `showJoke` function.
- The `showJoke` function selects a random joke from the list and updates the text of a `<div>` element with the ID `joke` to display the selected joke.
- Uses basic CSS for styling to make the joke display area more visually appealing.

You can save this code as an HTML file and open it in a web browser to see it in action.

To change the color of the jokes to red using CSS, you can modify the existing CSS rule for the `#joke` element to include a `color` property set to `red`. Here's how you can do it:

```css
