    margin: 20px;
    padding: 10px;
    border: 1px solid #ddd;
    border-radius: 5px;
    box-shadow: 0 0 10px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);
    color: red; /* Add this line to change the text color to red */
}
```

Alternatively, if you want to apply this style only when the joke is displayed (i.e., after the button is clicked), you could add a class to the `#joke` element in the JavaScript code and define the CSS for that class. Here's how you could do it:

```html
<style>
    #joke {
        margin: 20px;
        padding: 10px;
        border: 1px solid #ddd;
        border-radius: 5px;
        box-shadow: 0 0 10px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);
    }
    #joke.show {
        color: red;
    }
</style>
```

And then modify the JavaScript to add the `show` class when the joke is displayed:

```javascript
function showJoke() {
    const jokeElement = document.getElementById('joke');
    const randomJoke = jokes[Math.floor(Math.random() * jokes.length)];
    jokeElement.innerText = randomJoke;
    jokeElement.classList.add('show'); // Add this line
}
```

However, if you want the joke to remain red even after another joke is displayed, the first approach is simpler and more straightforward.

